"78_exceptions.mst — E0 + E1 of docs/exceptions_design.md.

 E0: the class hierarchy — Exception, Error and its concrete kinds, Warning.
 E1: `on:do:` and `signal`, so a library can finally report a recoverable
 condition to its caller instead of calling `self error:` and killing the
 computation.

 ── How this works, and why it needs no VM change ──────────────────────────

 The design doc's §3 proposed a new frame-marker predicate in the unwinder.
 Building E1 showed that is not necessary for the non-resumable case, because
 MACVM already has the two pieces the job needs:

   * a HANDLER STACK can live in the world (this file's `Handlers`), so
     `signal` finds its handler by SEARCHING, without touching frames; and
   * NON-LOCAL RETURN already unwinds correctly from an arbitrary depth back
     to a specific home frame, running every `ensure:`/`ifCurtailed:` block
     in between — which is exactly what `on:do:` must do once a handler
     decides to return.

 So `on:do:` pushes `[ :ex | ^aHandlerBlock value: ex ]` — a block whose HOME
 is the `on:do:` activation. `signal` calls it; the `^` inside performs an
 ordinary non-local return out of `on:do:`, and the existing unwinder does
 the rest. No new primitive, no new marker kind, no change to the JIT's
 `PRIM_ACTIVATES_FRAME` set.

 It is also NOT one-pass, which matters for the future: the SEARCH does not
 unwind. `signal` finds the handler and calls it with the stack intact, so a
 handler that answers WITHOUT a `^` simply returns its value to `signal` —
 which is resumption (E3), reachable later without redesigning this.

 ── What is deliberately NOT here (E4) ─────────────────────────────────────

 The VM's own failures — `self error:`, doesNotUnderstand, division by zero —
 do NOT signal yet. They still terminate exactly as before, so this file is
 purely additive and every existing gate keeps its meaning. `on: ZeroDivide
 do: […]` therefore catches a ZeroDivide someone SIGNALS, not yet one the VM
 raises. Wiring those is E4, and it must keep the no-handler path
 byte-identical to today."

Object subclass: Exception [
    | messageText |
    <classVars: Handlers>

    "── the handler stack ────────────────────────────────────────────────
     One entry per active `on:do:`, innermost LAST. Held on Exception (the
     root) so every subclass shares one stack — a handler for Error must be
     found by a ZeroDivide signalled deeper in."

    Exception class >> handlers [
        Handlers isNil ifTrue: [ Handlers := OrderedCollection new ].
        ^Handlers
    ]

    "Push (exceptionClass, block) and answer the depth to unwind to. The
     DEPTH, not an index into a list that may have grown — `popHandlerTo:`
     truncates back to it, so an abandoned frame's entries cannot leak even
     if a non-local return skipped their own pops."
    Exception class >> pushHandler: aClass block: aBlock [
        self handlers add: (Array with: aClass with: aBlock).
        ^self handlers size - 1
    ]

    "Truncate back to `aDepth`. Idempotent and total: called from an
     `ensure:`, so it must be correct after a normal return, after a
     non-local return through it, and after a curtailment."
    Exception class >> popHandlerTo: aDepth [
        Handlers isNil ifTrue: [ ^self ].
        [ Handlers size > aDepth ] whileTrue: [ Handlers removeLast ]
    ]

    "The innermost handler whose class this exception is a kind of, as
     (index, block), or nil. Searched innermost-first — the ANSI rule."
    Exception class >> findHandlerFor: anException [
        | hs i |
        hs := self handlers.
        i := hs size.
        [ i >= 1 ] whileTrue: [
            | entry |
            entry := hs at: i.
            (anException isKindOf: (entry at: 1)) ifTrue: [
                ^Array with: i with: (entry at: 2) ].
            i := i - 1 ].
        ^nil
    ]

    "── construction and text ────────────────────────────────────────────"

    Exception class >> new ^ <Exception> [ ^self basicNew ]

    "Signal a fresh instance of me — `ZeroDivide signal` and
     `Error signal: 'text'` are the two forms library code uses."
    Exception class >> signal ^ <Object> [ ^self new signal ]
    Exception class >> signal: aString <String> ^ <Object> [
        ^(self new messageText: aString) signal
    ]

    messageText ^ <Object> [ ^messageText ]
    messageText: aString <String> ^ <Exception> [ messageText := aString. ^self ]

    "What a report shows. Subclasses override `description` for a default
     when no messageText was given; messageText always wins if present."
    description ^ <String> [ ^'An exception occurred' ]

    printOn: aStream [
        aStream nextPutAll: self class name asString.
        messageText isNil ifFalse: [
            aStream nextPutAll: ': '; nextPutAll: messageText ]
    ]

    "The text a report should show: the specific message when one was given,
     else the class's own description."
    report ^ <String> [
        ^messageText isNil ifTrue: [ self description ] ifFalse: [ messageText ]
    ]

    "── signalling ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
     Search innermost-first for a handler. While it runs, the handler and
     everything inside it are POPPED, so a signal raised by the handler
     itself cannot re-enter the same handler (an infinite loop) and instead
     finds the next one outward — the ANSI rule, and the same shape the
     unwinder's `curtailing_on_error` guard uses for `ensure:`.

     The handler block is `on:do:`'s wrapper, whose `^` returns from the
     `on:do:` activation; so in the ordinary case this send does not return
     here at all. If the handler answers WITHOUT a non-local return, its
     value is answered from `signal` — the resumption path (E3)."
    signal ^ <Object> [
        | found depth block result |
        found := Exception findHandlerFor: self.
        found isNil ifTrue: [ ^self unhandled ].
        depth := (found at: 1) - 1.
        block := found at: 2.
        result := [ Exception popHandlerTo: depth. block value: self ]
                      ensure: [ ].
        ^result
    ]

    "No handler matched. v1 keeps EXACTLY the old behaviour — the computation
     stops with a reported error — so a world that signals but never handles
     is no worse off than one that called `self error:` directly, and every
     existing gate keeps its meaning."
    unhandled ^ <Object> [
        "asString on the class name: it is a Symbol, and Symbols are
         immutable — , would try to build in place and raise inside the
         error reporter itself (found by testing the unhandled path)."
        ^self error: self class name asString , ': ' , self report
    ]

    "Is the receiver resumable? Nothing resumes in E1; the accessor exists so
     E3 has one place to change and so handlers can ask honestly."
    isResumable ^ <Boolean> [ ^false ]
]

"── the standard kinds ───────────────────────────────────────────────────
 Deliberately small. Each exists because the world's own library already
 fails in exactly this way and currently has no way to say so."

Exception subclass: Error [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'An error occurred' ]
]

Error subclass: ZeroDivide [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Division by zero' ]
]

Error subclass: MessageNotUnderstood [
    | message receiver |
    message ^ <Object> [ ^message ]
    message: m <Object> ^ <MessageNotUnderstood> [ message := m. ^self ]
    receiver ^ <Object> [ ^receiver ]
    receiver: r <Object> ^ <MessageNotUnderstood> [ receiver := r. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Message not understood' ]
]

Error subclass: KeyNotFound [
    | key |
    key ^ <Object> [ ^key ]
    key: k <Object> ^ <KeyNotFound> [ key := k. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Key not found' ]
]

Error subclass: IndexOutOfBounds [
    | index |
    index ^ <Object> [ ^index ]
    index: i <Object> ^ <IndexOutOfBounds> [ index := i. ^self ]
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Index out of bounds' ]
]

Error subclass: InvalidArgument [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Invalid argument' ]
]

"Resumable by design — the ANSI distinction that keeps the dangerous case
 opt-in. Nothing resumes until E3; `isResumable` answering true here is the
 declaration, not the mechanism."
Exception subclass: Warning [
    description ^ <String> [ ^'Warning' ]
    isResumable ^ <Boolean> [ ^true ]
]

"── E1: the protected block ──────────────────────────────────────────────"

"Re-opened, the world's own idiom for adding methods to an existing class
 (59_reflection.mst does the same) — a redeclaration carrying only the new
 method, not a redefinition."
Object subclass: BlockClosure [
    "Run me, and if I signal an exception that is a kind of `anExceptionClass`,
     run `aHandlerBlock` with it and answer ITS value from this `on:do:`.

     The pushed wrapper's `^` is what does the returning: its home is THIS
     activation, so the existing non-local-return unwinder walks back from
     wherever the signal happened, running every `ensure:`/`ifCurtailed:`
     block in between, and lands here. The `ensure:` below pops the handler
     on every exit path — normal, non-local, or curtailed."
    on: anExceptionClass <Object> do: aHandlerBlock <[Object,^Object]> ^ <Object> [
        | depth |
        depth := Exception pushHandler: anExceptionClass
                           block: [ :ex | ^aHandlerBlock value: ex ].
        ^[ self value ] ensure: [ Exception popHandlerTo: depth ]
    ]
]
